    "body": "### Problem\n\nWhen checking to see if the cursor is inside of a `vim.Range`, the inclusive nature of `vim.Range:has()` will incorrectly include positions where the cursor is directly after the bounds of the range, since the end column will be the same as the cursor's column:\n\n```lua\nlocal range = vim.range.lsp(0, lsp_range, position_encoding)\nrange:has(vim.pos.cursor()) -- Logic error: Will include positions where `vim.pos.cursor().col == range.end_col`!\n```\n\nThe best way I've come up with is this, which is very verbose:\n\n```lua\nlocal range = vim.range.lsp(0, lsp_range, position_encoding)\nrange = vim.range(range.buf, range.start_row, range.start_col, range.end_row, range.end_col - 1)\nrange:has(vim.pos.cursor()) \n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nProvide some method to deal with this in code. Potential solutions:\n\n```lua\n--- Modifiable ranges\nlocal range = vim.range.lsp(0, lsp_range, position_encoding)\nrange.end_col = range.end_col - 1\nrange:has(vim.pos.cursor())\n\n-- Method to create a new range from offsets\nlocal range = vim.range.lsp(0, lsp_range, position_encoding)\nrange:shift(0, 0, 0, -1):has(vim.pos.cursor())\n\n-- Add option to Range:has()\nlocal range = vim.range.lsp(0, lsp_range, position_encoding)\nrange:has(vim.pos.cursor(), { end_exclusive = true ))\n```\n\n @ofseed @TheLeoP",
